# Security headers for every page Netlify serves. Vite copies this file into dist/.
# The content security policy only reports for now, and only to each visitor's own
# browser console: it names no report-to collector, so nobody receives the reports.
# Before enforcing, add a collector and let it run quiet on the live site, then
# rename the header to Content-Security-Policy and add frame-ancestors 'none'
# (browsers ignore frame-ancestors in a report-only policy, which is why
# X-Frame-Options stays). Deploy previews always log two reports from Netlify's own
# toolbar script; ignore those. tests/e2e/securityHeaders.spec.ts loads every page
# under this policy and fails on any violation.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://formspree.io; frame-src https://maps.google.com https://www.google.com; form-action 'self' https://formspree.io; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'
